## What to extract

**Entity** — something with a name and an identity that the document talks
about. A feature, a system, a team, a person, a customer. Give each one a
`local_key` such as `e1`, `e2`. Never use a key that starts with `m` followed
by a number; those are reserved for the already-known entities above.

**Claim** — a statement about the *value* of some property of one entity.

    subject   the entity's local_key
    predicate the property being described, in snake_case
    value     a single literal

**RelationAssertion** — a statement connecting *two entities*.

    payment_feature --depends_on--> auth_system

## The value must be a literal

This is the rule that decides whether something is a claim at all.

A value is one date, one number, one boolean, one short label. If what you
want to write is a sentence, it is not a claim.

    good   payment_feature · release_month · "2026-05"
    good   slack_ingestion · min_title_length · 10
    bad    payment_feature · has_bug · "Sync가 안되는 버그"
    bad    slack_ingestion · embedding_policy · "제목이 10글자 이하면 임베딩 안 함"

When you catch yourself writing a sentence into `value`, do one of these
instead.

1. Find the literal hiding inside it. "제목이 10글자 이하면 임베딩 안 함"
   carries the number 10, so report `min_title_length · 10`.
2. If it describes how two things relate, make it a RelationAssertion.
3. If neither works, leave it out. A missing claim costs less than a claim
   that cannot be compared with anything.

`value_type` must be one of `date`, `number`, `boolean`, `enum`, `text`.
Reach for `text` last, and keep it under about 40 characters.

## What not to extract

The document may display the source system's own record fields — ticket
status, priority, type, assignee, sprint, issue key, URL, branch names,
commit counts, review dates. **Do not turn these into claims.** They are
structured data that the system already holds outside the text, and
recording them here only creates duplicates that drift.

Extract what the document *says*, not what the source system *stores about*
the document.

## Rules

- Every claim's `subject_local_key` and every relation's endpoints must refer
  either to an entity you return in `entities`, or to one of the already-known
  entities listed above. Never point at anything else.
- Reuse one predicate for one property. If you write `release_month` once, do
  not write `launch_month` for the same idea later.
- `statement` must quote the exact span of the document that supports the
  claim. Do not paraphrase it; the server locates this text in the source.
- Extract only what this document states. Do not add what you know from
  elsewhere, and do not resolve contradictions you find.
{%- if reference_time %}
- The document's reference time is << reference_time >>. Interpret every
  relative time expression ("next month", "어제", "다음 달 15일") against
  this reference time and write absolute values. Dates must use the
  `YYYY`, `YYYY-MM`, or `YYYY-MM-DD` form only. If a time expression
  cannot be resolved to an absolute date, omit that value (or the claim
  if the time is its point).
- If the document states a time bound for a claim, set `valid_from` or
  `valid_to`, resolved the same way. Unlike a claim's value, a bound must
  resolve to a full calendar date (`YYYY-MM-DD`) or a full timestamp — if
  you can only determine the year or the month, leave the bound empty.
{%- else %}
- If the document states a time bound for a claim, set `valid_from` or
  `valid_to`. A bound must be a full calendar date (`YYYY-MM-DD`) or a
  full timestamp — if you can only determine the year or the month, leave
  the bound empty. Leave them empty otherwise. Do not guess dates for
